Implement the in-process synchronisation backend on macOS.

Upstream added the whole fast-path synchronisation framework in wine-10.15
(server/inproc_sync.c, the get_sync object op, the client-side handle cache in
dlls/ntdll/unix/sync.c) but the only backend is Linux's /dev/ntsync, so on macOS
every wait still costs a wineserver round trip.  This fills the slot upstream
left open rather than adding a parallel msync/esync of our own: the per-object
providers that the old WIP patch was missing already exist as get_sync, and are
already wired to events, mutexes, semaphores and the thread alert.

The server gives each object a page of shared memory from shm_open(), unlinks the
name immediately and passes the descriptor to the client through the existing
get_inproc_sync_fd reply, so the protocol is unchanged.  The client maps that page
and runs the state machine in userspace, blocking on __ulock_wait with
UL_COMPARE_AND_WAIT_SHARED.  The state machine itself lives in include/wine/macsync.h
as inline helpers so that wineserver and ntdll cannot drift apart.

A thread cannot block on several addresses at once, so a wait on more than one
object blocks on a single process-wide counter that every state change bumps and
rechecks the objects on each wake, while a wait on one object blocks on that
object's own counter and is never woken by unrelated traffic.  Both counters carry
a waiter count so an uncontended signal never enters the kernel at all.

Two things worth knowing for anyone touching this:

  - a shared __ulock_wait on a page that is mapped but not yet resident fails with
    EFAULT instead of blocking, so mac_map_page() touches the page once after mmap.
  - WaitAll checks every object before it consumes any, and rolls back if it loses
    a race.  That is not perfectly atomic the way ntsync is in-kernel, but it is
    what fsync does and Windows leaves the difference observable anyway.

Off by default, opt in with WINEMSYNC=1.  Without it get_inproc_device_fd() returns
-1, every object is created as a plain server object and none of this runs.

Measured on wine-11.0, 20000 iterations of each:

  event set + wait, uncontended    16660 ns -> 2071 ns
  event ping-pong, two threads     21120 ns -> 5950 ns
  semaphore release + acquire      16593 ns -> 2026 ns
  mutex acquire + release          16738 ns -> 1997 ns
  WaitForMultipleObjects, 4 objs   16784 ns -> 2077 ns

Applies clean to wine-11.0 and wine-11.14.

